theory.intro:
A quadrilateral can have more than one name. Check the properties each name requires, rather than how the shape looks. Use all the names guaranteed by the information, or the most specific name if the question asks for it.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\normalsize,text=black}]
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (Q) at (0,8) {Quadrilateral};
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (K) at (-4.2,6.2) {Kite};
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (T) at (2.2,6.2) {Trapezium};
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (P) at (2.2,4.4) {Parallelogram};
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (Rh) at (-2,2.4) {Rhombus};
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (Re) at (4.4,2.4) {Rectangle};
\node[draw,rounded corners,inner sep=6pt,minimum width=6cm,minimum height=0.9cm] (Sq) at (1.2,0.4) {Square};
\draw[black,->] (Q)--(K);
\draw[black,->] (Q)--(T);
\draw[black,->] (T)--(P);
\draw[black,->] (K)--(Rh);
\draw[black,->] (P)--(Rh);
\draw[black,->] (P)--(Re);
\draw[black,->] (Rh)--(Sq);
\draw[black,->] (Re)--(Sq);
\node at (0.4,-1) {Each arrow points to a more specific type.};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

